$ the transformation $T$ is a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491115 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ if and only if ${v}_{1}={v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491117 1071 "Expressed in its simplest form, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\,\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\,}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}=\n$${{field-1}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491117 1071 "Expressed in its simplest form, $\\frac{\\,\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\,}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}=${{field-1}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491133 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and $v\\ne 0,$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection and the matrix $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491133 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and $v\\ne 0,$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection and the matrix $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491183 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491183 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491184 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\,\\,(-4)\\,\\,}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491184 1071 "$\\frac{\\,\\,(-4)\\,\\,}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491190 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491190 1071 "$\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491208 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.$\n\n![](images/72/7280ccde2bcdd9fab928ddff23dc0da5e48c75920ddd1ce2566dcb1c113a226e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}cx{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}cx{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{c}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}((2-y)^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}(4-4y+{y}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c}{2}(4{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2c({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2c({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y & =2c{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2c[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491208 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.$\n\n![](images/72/7280ccde2bcdd9fab928ddff23dc0da5e48c75920ddd1ce2566dcb1c113a226e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}cx{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}cx{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{c}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}((2-y)^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}(4-4y+{y}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{c}{2}(4{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2c({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2c({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y & =2c{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2c[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491217 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left(-\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}{(-8)}=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491217 1071 "$\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}{(-8)}=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491292 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\sinh (4x - 5)dx\n$$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491292 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\sinh (4x - 5)dx$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491301 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\sinh (4x - 2)dx\n$$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491301 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\sinh (4x - 2)dx$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491305 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh \\left(\\frac{2x - 1}{10}\\right)dx.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186491305 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh \\left(\\frac{2x - 1}{10}\\right)dx$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186491306 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\sinh (6x - 5)dx\n$$?" 13194139539