al components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=17$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186514438 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=17$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186514444 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-0.5x+0.5xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=-0.5+0.5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=0.5x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-5xy)=1-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5y & 0.5x \\\\ -5y & 1-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5(1) & 0.5(0.2) \\\\ -5(1) & 1-5(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-5)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n